{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences: shortcuts where you press keys one after another rather than simultaneously. Common uses are Vim-style navigation, cheat codes, and multi-step commands."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']"},{"type":"text","value":" follows a physical position, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['G', 'G']"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']"},{"type":"text","value":". Display steps with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic usage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" primitive to register a key sequence:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  // Vim-style: press g then g to scroll to top\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n    window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n  })\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first argument is an array of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Hotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" strings representing each step in the sequence. The user must press them in order within the timeout window."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"many-sequences-at-once","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many sequences at once"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For several sequences or a dynamic list, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeySequences"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of many "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" calls. Pass a plain array or an accessor that returns definitions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\ncreateHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options merge like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults, then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commonOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", then each definition's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options"},{"type":"text","value":". For element-scoped multi-sequence registration, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeySequencesAttachment"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactive-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence options support the same "},{"type":"link","href":"./hotkeys.md#property-getters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"property getter patterns"}]},{"type":"text","value":" as hotkey options, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"timeout"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target"},{"type":"text","value":". Updates preserve the registration when its sequence, target, and platform stay the same."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts accessor functions for reactive sequence and options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = createSignal(true)\nconst [sequence] = createSignal(['G', 'G'] as const)\n\ncreateHotkeySequence(\n  sequence,\n  () => scrollToTop(),\n  () => ({ enabled: isVimMode(), timeout: 1500 }),\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sequence-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The third argument is an options object (or accessor returning options):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,  // Time allowed between keys (ms)\n  enabled: true,  // Whether the sequence is active\n  target: document, // Or from an accessor for scoped sequences\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"timeout","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"timeout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The maximum time (in milliseconds) allowed between consecutive key presses. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1000"},{"type":"text","value":" (1 second)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine(), { timeout: 500 })\ncreateHotkeySequence(['Shift+Z', 'Shift+Z'], () => forceQuit(), { timeout: 2000 })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enabled","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls whether the sequence is active. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". Use an accessor for reactive control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabled sequences remain registered and stay visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = createSignal(true)\n\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), () => ({\n  enabled: isVimMode(),\n}))","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"target","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The DOM element to attach the sequence listener to. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"document"},{"type":"text","value":". Can be from an accessor when the target becomes available after mount."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global defaults via provider"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"meta","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequences support the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" option as hotkeys. Attach a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":" for use in shortcut palettes and devtools."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkeys Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sequences-with-modifiers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequences with modifiers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each step in a sequence can include modifiers:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(['Mod+K', 'Mod+C'], () => commentSelection())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"chained-modifier-chords","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chained modifier chords"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can repeat the same modifier across consecutive steps, for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+R"},{"type":"text","value":" then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+T"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => {\n  doNextAction()\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"modifier-only-keys-between-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modifier-only keys between steps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone, with no letter or other key) are ignored. They neither advance the sequence nor reset progress, so a user can tap or hold Shift between chords without breaking the sequence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"common-sequence-patterns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common sequence patterns"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"vim-style-navigation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vim-style navigation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function VimNavigation() {\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n  createHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\n  createHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\n  createHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"konami-code","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Konami Code"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(\n  [\n    'ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp',\n    'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'B', 'A',\n  ],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"multi-step-commands","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-step commands"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(['H', 'E', 'L', 'P'], () => openHelp())","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-sequences-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How sequences work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSequenceMatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":" (singleton) handles all sequence registrations. When a key is pressed:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It checks if the key matches the next expected step in any registered sequence"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If it matches, the sequence advances to the next step"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the timeout expires between steps, the sequence resets"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When all steps are completed, the callback fires"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modifier-only keydowns are ignored (they neither advance nor reset the sequence)"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"overlapping-sequences","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overlapping sequences"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple sequences can share the same prefix. The manager tracks progress for each sequence independently:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'I', 'W'], () => deleteInnerWord())","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"conflicting-registrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conflicting registrations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-sequence-manager","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sequence manager"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Under the hood, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the singleton "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":". You can also use the core "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSequenceMatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" function for standalone sequence matching:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createSequenceMatcher } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], { timeout: 1000 })\n\ndocument.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {\n  if (matcher.match(e)) {\n    console.log('Sequence completed!')\n  }\n  console.log('Progress:', matcher.getProgress())\n})","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic usage","level":2},{"id":"many-sequences-at-once","text":"Many sequences at once","level":2},{"id":"reactive-options","text":"Reactive options","level":2},{"id":"sequence-options","text":"Sequence options","level":2},{"id":"timeout","text":"timeout","level":3},{"id":"enabled","text":"enabled","level":3},{"id":"target","text":"target","level":3},{"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","text":"Global defaults via provider","level":3},{"id":"meta","text":"meta","level":3},{"id":"sequences-with-modifiers","text":"Sequences with modifiers","level":2},{"id":"chained-modifier-chords","text":"Chained modifier chords","level":2},{"id":"modifier-only-keys-between-steps","text":"Modifier-only keys between steps","level":3},{"id":"common-sequence-patterns","text":"Common sequence patterns","level":2},{"id":"vim-style-navigation","text":"Vim-style navigation","level":3},{"id":"konami-code","text":"Konami Code","level":3},{"id":"multi-step-commands","text":"Multi-step commands","level":3},{"id":"how-sequences-work","text":"How sequences work","level":2},{"id":"overlapping-sequences","text":"Overlapping sequences","level":3},{"id":"conflicting-registrations","text":"Conflicting registrations","level":3},{"id":"the-sequence-manager","text":"The sequence manager","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences: shortcuts where you press keys one after another rather than simultaneously. Common uses are Vim-style navigation, cheat codes, and multi-step commands.\n\nSequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, `['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']` follows a physical position, while `['G', 'G']` follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as `['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']`. Display steps with `sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')`.\n\n## Basic usage\n\nUse the `createHotkeySequence` primitive to register a key sequence:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  // Vim-style: press g then g to scroll to top\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n    window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe first argument is an array of `Hotkey` strings representing each step in the sequence. The user must press them in order within the timeout window.\n\n## Many sequences at once\n\nFor several sequences or a dynamic list, use `createHotkeySequences` instead of many `createHotkeySequence` calls. Pass a plain array or an accessor that returns definitions.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\ncreateHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])\n```\n\nOptions merge like `createHotkeys`: `HotkeysProvider` defaults, then `commonOptions`, then each definition's `options`. For element-scoped multi-sequence registration, use `createHotkeySequencesAttachment`.\n\n## Reactive options\n\nSequence options support the same [property getter patterns](./hotkeys.md#property-getters) as hotkey options, including `enabled`, `timeout`, and `target`. Updates preserve the registration when its sequence, target, and platform stay the same.\n\nSolid's `createHotkeySequence` accepts accessor functions for reactive sequence and options:\n\n```tsx\nconst [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = createSignal(true)\nconst [sequence] = createSignal(['G', 'G'] as const)\n\ncreateHotkeySequence(\n  sequence,\n  () => scrollToTop(),\n  () => ({ enabled: isVimMode(), timeout: 1500 }),\n)\n```\n\n## Sequence options\n\nThe third argument is an options object (or accessor returning options):\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,  // Time allowed between keys (ms)\n  enabled: true,  // Whether the sequence is active\n  target: document, // Or from an accessor for scoped sequences\n})\n```\n\n### `timeout`\n\nThe maximum time (in milliseconds) allowed between consecutive key presses. Defaults to `1000` (1 second).\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine(), { timeout: 500 })\ncreateHotkeySequence(['Shift+Z', 'Shift+Z'], () => forceQuit(), { timeout: 2000 })\n```\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the sequence is active. Defaults to `true`. Use an accessor for reactive control.\n\nDisabled sequences remain registered and stay visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = createSignal(true)\n\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), () => ({\n  enabled: isVimMode(),\n}))\n```\n\n### `target`\n\nThe DOM element to attach the sequence listener to. Defaults to `document`. Can be from an accessor when the target becomes available after mount.\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n### `meta`\n\nSequences support the same `meta` option as hotkeys. Attach a `name` and `description` for use in shortcut palettes and devtools.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Hotkeys Guide](./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description) for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations.\n\n## Sequences with modifiers\n\nEach step in a sequence can include modifiers:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['Mod+K', 'Mod+C'], () => commentSelection())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n```\n\n## Chained modifier chords\n\nThis example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters.\n\nYou can repeat the same modifier across consecutive steps, for example `Shift+R` then `Shift+T`:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => {\n  doNextAction()\n})\n```\n\n### Modifier-only keys between steps\n\nWhile a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone, with no letter or other key) are ignored. They neither advance the sequence nor reset progress, so a user can tap or hold Shift between chords without breaking the sequence.\n\n## Common sequence patterns\n\n### Vim-style navigation\n\n```tsx\nfunction VimNavigation() {\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n  createHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\n  createHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\n  createHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n}\n```\n\n### Konami Code\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(\n  [\n    'ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp',\n    'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'B', 'A',\n  ],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)\n```\n\n### Multi-step commands\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['H', 'E', 'L', 'P'], () => openHelp())\n```\n\n## How sequences work\n\nBoth `SequenceManager` and `createSequenceMatcher` ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches.\n\nThe `SequenceManager` (singleton) handles all sequence registrations. When a key is pressed:\n\n1. It checks if the key matches the next expected step in any registered sequence\n2. If it matches, the sequence advances to the next step\n3. If the timeout expires between steps, the sequence resets\n4. When all steps are completed, the callback fires\n5. Modifier-only keydowns are ignored (they neither advance nor reset the sequence)\n\n### Overlapping sequences\n\nMultiple sequences can share the same prefix. The manager tracks progress for each sequence independently:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'I', 'W'], () => deleteInnerWord())\n```\n\n### Conflicting registrations\n\nFor the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. `conflictBehavior` applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap.\n\n## The sequence manager\n\nUnder the hood, `createHotkeySequence` uses the singleton `SequenceManager`. You can also use the core `createSequenceMatcher` function for standalone sequence matching:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createSequenceMatcher } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], { timeout: 1000 })\n\ndocument.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {\n  if (matcher.match(e)) {\n    console.log('Sequence completed!')\n  }\n  console.log('Progress:', matcher.getProgress())\n})\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Sequences Guide\nid: sequences\n---\n\nTanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences: shortcuts where you press keys one after another rather than simultaneously. Common uses are Vim-style navigation, cheat codes, and multi-step commands.\n\nSequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, `['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']` follows a physical position, while `['G', 'G']` follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as `['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']`. Display steps with `sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')`.\n\n## Basic usage\n\nUse the `createHotkeySequence` primitive to register a key sequence:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  // Vim-style: press g then g to scroll to top\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n    window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe first argument is an array of `Hotkey` strings representing each step in the sequence. The user must press them in order within the timeout window.\n\n## Many sequences at once\n\nFor several sequences or a dynamic list, use `createHotkeySequences` instead of many `createHotkeySequence` calls. Pass a plain array or an accessor that returns definitions.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\ncreateHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])\n```\n\nOptions merge like `createHotkeys`: `HotkeysProvider` defaults, then `commonOptions`, then each definition's `options`. For element-scoped multi-sequence registration, use `createHotkeySequencesAttachment`.\n\n## Reactive options\n\nSequence options support the same [property getter patterns](./hotkeys.md#property-getters) as hotkey options, including `enabled`, `timeout`, and `target`. Updates preserve the registration when its sequence, target, and platform stay the same.\n\nSolid's `createHotkeySequence` accepts accessor functions for reactive sequence and options:\n\n```tsx\nconst [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = createSignal(true)\nconst [sequence] = createSignal(['G', 'G'] as const)\n\ncreateHotkeySequence(\n  sequence,\n  () => scrollToTop(),\n  () => ({ enabled: isVimMode(), timeout: 1500 }),\n)\n```\n\n## Sequence options\n\nThe third argument is an options object (or accessor returning options):\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,  // Time allowed between keys (ms)\n  enabled: true,  // Whether the sequence is active\n  target: document, // Or from an accessor for scoped sequences\n})\n```\n\n### `timeout`\n\nThe maximum time (in milliseconds) allowed between consecutive key presses. Defaults to `1000` (1 second).\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine(), { timeout: 500 })\ncreateHotkeySequence(['Shift+Z', 'Shift+Z'], () => forceQuit(), { timeout: 2000 })\n```\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the sequence is active. Defaults to `true`. Use an accessor for reactive control.\n\nDisabled sequences remain registered and stay visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = createSignal(true)\n\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), () => ({\n  enabled: isVimMode(),\n}))\n```\n\n### `target`\n\nThe DOM element to attach the sequence listener to. Defaults to `document`. Can be from an accessor when the target becomes available after mount.\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n### `meta`\n\nSequences support the same `meta` option as hotkeys. Attach a `name` and `description` for use in shortcut palettes and devtools.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Hotkeys Guide](./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description) for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations.\n\n## Sequences with modifiers\n\nEach step in a sequence can include modifiers:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['Mod+K', 'Mod+C'], () => commentSelection())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n```\n\n## Chained modifier chords\n\nThis example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters.\n\nYou can repeat the same modifier across consecutive steps, for example `Shift+R` then `Shift+T`:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => {\n  doNextAction()\n})\n```\n\n### Modifier-only keys between steps\n\nWhile a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone, with no letter or other key) are ignored. They neither advance the sequence nor reset progress, so a user can tap or hold Shift between chords without breaking the sequence.\n\n## Common sequence patterns\n\n### Vim-style navigation\n\n```tsx\nfunction VimNavigation() {\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\n  createHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n  createHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\n  createHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\n  createHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n}\n```\n\n### Konami Code\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(\n  [\n    'ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp',\n    'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'B', 'A',\n  ],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)\n```\n\n### Multi-step commands\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['H', 'E', 'L', 'P'], () => openHelp())\n```\n\n## How sequences work\n\nBoth `SequenceManager` and `createSequenceMatcher` ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches.\n\nThe `SequenceManager` (singleton) handles all sequence registrations. When a key is pressed:\n\n1. It checks if the key matches the next expected step in any registered sequence\n2. If it matches, the sequence advances to the next step\n3. If the timeout expires between steps, the sequence resets\n4. When all steps are completed, the callback fires\n5. Modifier-only keydowns are ignored (they neither advance nor reset the sequence)\n\n### Overlapping sequences\n\nMultiple sequences can share the same prefix. The manager tracks progress for each sequence independently:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\ncreateHotkeySequence(['D', 'I', 'W'], () => deleteInnerWord())\n```\n\n### Conflicting registrations\n\nFor the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. `conflictBehavior` applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap.\n\n## The sequence manager\n\nUnder the hood, `createHotkeySequence` uses the singleton `SequenceManager`. You can also use the core `createSequenceMatcher` function for standalone sequence matching:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createSequenceMatcher } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], { timeout: 1000 })\n\ndocument.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {\n  if (matcher.match(e)) {\n    console.log('Sequence completed!')\n  }\n  console.log('Progress:', matcher.getProgress())\n})\n```\n"}
